# =============================================================================
# kairos settings — SEPARATE RECORDING-PC setup (two machines).
# Use this ONLY to record from a different PC than the robot, so the robot is
# not loaded. Running everything on ONE PC? Use .env.example instead.
#
#   1) On the RECORDING PC:  cp .env.split.example .env
#   2) Edit ONE line: set ROBOT_IP to the robot's LAN IP (everything else
#      references it). Then start this PC's services:
#         make recording-up            # or: docker compose -f compose.recording.yaml up -d --build
#   3) On the ROBOT, separately run:   make robot-up
#
# This PC runs orchestrator/dora/frontend, joins NO DDS, and reaches the
# robot-edge services (recorder/monitor/streamer/probe — or recorder/dora_live
# under LIVE=1, see the LIVE block below) over HTTP at ROBOT_IP.
# Only lightweight data crosses the LAN — the robot's onboard system is not loaded.
# Walkthrough: README ("Which .env do I use?"). Rationale: docs/specs/ja/deployment_topology.md.
# =============================================================================

# ---- The robot's LAN IP (the one place to edit) -----------------------------
# Use an IP (or an /etc/hosts entry). nginx bakes these into static upstreams at
# container start, so a hostname is resolved once at nginx startup.
#   export ROBOT_IP=192.168.1.50   # then the *_HOST below pick it up
ROBOT_IP=192.168.1.50

# ---- Downstream service hosts (orchestrator -> robot-edge services) ----------
# The four DDS-reading services run on the robot; point the orchestrator at them.
RECORDER_HOST=${ROBOT_IP}
TOPIC_MONITOR_HOST=${ROBOT_IP}
WEBRTC_HOST=${ROBOT_IP}
TOPIC_PROBE_HOST=${ROBOT_IP}
# dora_runner is CPU-heavy and runs on THIS PC beside the orchestrator.
DORA_RUNNER_HOST=localhost

# ---- nginx upstream hosts (frontend -> robot-edge services) ------------------
# The frontend is on this PC; the orchestrator is co-located here (API_HOST), but
# the streamer + probe are on the robot. NOTE: nginx proxies only the WebRTC
# *signaling*; RTP media is peer-to-peer, so the browser must reach ROBOT_IP
# directly (same-LAN premise — satisfied on a wired LAN).
API_HOST=127.0.0.1
WEBRTC_HOST=${ROBOT_IP}
PROBE_HOST=${ROBOT_IP}

# ---- Ports (must match the robot's; defaults shown) -------------------------
API_ORCH_PORT=8000
TOPIC_MONITOR_PORT=8001
WEBRTC_PORT=8002
TOPIC_PROBE_PORT=8003
RECORDER_PORT=8010
DORA_RUNNER_PORT=8020
FRONTEND_PORT=8080

# ---- dora_live cutover (LIVE=1) ---------------------------------------------
# LIVE=1 replaces the robot's monitor/streamer/probe trio with dora_live ON THE
# ROBOT (same topology — live topics never cross the wire as DDS; this PC just
# proxies to the robot's 8005/8006/8007, ports `make` exports automatically).
# Set it HERE (both hosts read this file) so the cutover is sticky: `make
# robot-up` then runs recorder+dora_live, `make recording-up` keeps proxying to
# the dora_live ports. Remove/0 + re-run both `up`s to fall back to the trio.
# The live topic set / per-topic QoS / camera codecs (ffmpeg etc.) live in
# config/<robot>/live/default.yaml on the ROBOT (docs/specs/ja/dora_live.md).
# LIVE=1
# DORA_LIVE_CPUS=8   # opt-in: pin dora_live to the first 8 cpus (bounds per-bridge tokio threads + CPU on the robot's many-core host)

# ---- ROS 2 graph (takes effect on the ROBOT side) ---------------------------
# Use this same file as the ROBOT's .env too (`make robot-up` reads it). These
# keys drive the four DDS-reading services and MUST match the robot's ROS 2
# system; on the recording PC they are inert (orchestrator/dora/frontend never
# join DDS). ROS_DISTRO also picks the image tag/base — the .env value wins
# over the Makefile default.
ROS_DOMAIN_ID=0
ROS_DISTRO=jazzy
RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_fastrtps_cpp # RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_cyclonedds_cpp
# Cross-host / unicast DDS tuning (optional): point at files under the mounted
# /config (loaded via env_file, reaches the robot-edge containers).
# CYCLONEDDS_URI=file:///config/cyclonedds.xml
# FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=/config/fastdds-profiles.xml
# Custom-message overlay so the robot-edge services can record/monitor/probe
# custom types. `make` derives it from ROBOT when deploy/msgs_overlay/<robot>/
# exists; set it here only to override. MUST start with ./
# MSGS_OVERLAY_DIR=./deploy/msgs_overlay/<robot>
# The robot-edge HTTP APIs bind BIND_HOST (default 0.0.0.0) — keep it so this
# PC can reach them across the LAN. Narrow it to the robot's LAN interface IP
# only if you must.
# BIND_HOST=0.0.0.0

# ---- WebRTC ICE servers (STUN/TURN) — for the camera preview across networks -
# The split's data plane (metrics/record) works over any reachable path, but the
# WebRTC camera preview needs a DIRECT browser<->streamer path. Same-LAN or
# Tailscale (directly routable) needs nothing here. Across NAT / WiFi client
# isolation / the internet, set STUN and/or a TURN relay (see compose.turn.yaml).
# JSON array in browser RTCIceServer shape; browser + streamer both use it; a
# blank/malformed value degrades to "no ICE" (never crashes services).
#   WEBRTC_ICE_SERVERS=[{"urls":["stun:stun.l.google.com:19302"]},{"urls":["turn:${ROBOT_IP}:3478"],"username":"kairos","credential":"changeme"}]

# ---- Proxy exemption ---------------------------------------------------------
# If this PC sits behind a corporate proxy, Docker injects HTTP(S)_PROXY into
# every container and LAN calls to the robot would be routed to the proxy and
# fail. Every kairos call is LAN-internal — exempt localhost AND the robot.
# 169.254.0.0/16 (link-local) is included because a directly-cabled robot NIC
# without DHCP self-assigns an address in that range; a raw curl call to it
# hangs behind a proxy that doesn't know the address is local. Not every tool
# honors CIDR ranges in NO_PROXY (e.g. Python's urllib does plain suffix
# matching) — ${ROBOT_IP} above is the reliable exact-match fallback for those.
NO_PROXY=localhost,127.0.0.1,169.254.0.0/16,${ROBOT_IP}

# ---- Data root (PC-LOCAL) ---------------------------------------------------
# dora reads MCAP from here. `make import-runs` rsyncs COMPLETED runs from the
# robot into ./data/recorded. Do NOT NFS-mount the robot's /data here — dora
# scanning large MCAPs over NFS would make the robot serve disk/network.
DATA_DIR=./data

# ---- Sample-bag replay (deploy/test harness: make rosbag / rosbag-loop) ----
# Bag played by `make rosbag` / `make rosbag-loop`. Path is relative to data/
# (e.g. airoa-moma-mcap/000730); an absolute /data/... path also works. Unset =
# the bundled sample (airoa-moma-mcap/235210, see deploy/test/compose.yaml).
# BAG=airoa-moma-mcap/000730
# Uncomment to always loop (same effect as `make rosbag-loop`).
# LOOP=--loop

# ---- Robot / config (informational on this PC) ------------------------------
# The orchestrator/frontend use ROBOT to label + locate the config catalog. The
# recorder's recording behavior (start_paused, max_cache_size_mb, QoS) is driven
# by the ROBOT's /config (deploy-time), NOT by edits made here. To change recorder
# behavior, edit config/ on the robot and `make config-reload` there. A robot
# defined under the gitignored config/local/<robot>/ does not travel with git —
# publish this PC's copy to the robot's clone with `make push-config`, then
# `make robot-config-reload` on the robot.
ROBOT=airoa_hsr
# Used by import-runs (rsync source). Set to the robot's ssh user + data path.
ROBOT_SSH=robot@${ROBOT_IP}
ROBOT_DATA_DIR=/home/robot/kairos/data

# ---- Run transfer (importer sidecar + make import-runs) ----------------------
# Passwordless auth to the robot — set ONE of these (else ssh prompts and the
# importer's automated pulls fail):
#   ROBOT_SSH_PASSWORD  the robot user's password (easiest: write it and go —
#                       sshpass feeds it to ssh; the recording PC's compose
#                       ships sshpass, `make import-runs` on the host needs the
#                       sshpass package installed). Trusted-LAN posture: this
#                       file is gitignored, but it IS a plaintext password.
#   ROBOT_SSH_KEY       ABSOLUTE path to an ssh identity file (preferred).
#                       One-time setup:
#                         ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/kairos_robot -N ''
#                         ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/kairos_robot.pub robot@<ROBOT_IP>
# ROBOT_SSH_PASSWORD=
# ROBOT_SSH_KEY=/home/<you>/.ssh/kairos_robot
#
# Auto-pull on Collect Save is a CONFIG option, not an env: set
# transfer.auto_pull_on_save: true in config/<robot>/recording/default.yaml
# (Settings > Advanced JSON). Default off = nothing transfers on its own.
# The robot-side copy is always left in place.
#
# rsync bandwidth cap in KB/s (0 = unlimited). Recording is NOT affected by a
# concurrent pull (measured; deploy/test/overlap_eval) — cap it only to protect
# the WebRTC preview's share of a thin link (WiFi/Tailscale), e.g. 10000.
# BWLIMIT=0
# Importer catch-up sweep, seconds between full pulls (0 = off). Only useful
# with auto_pull_on_save: it re-tries pulls that failed (robot offline at Save)
# and picks up runs that never got a Save.
# IMPORT_SWEEP_S=0
# Importer HTTP trigger bind/port (localhost-only by default).
# IMPORTER_BIND=127.0.0.1
# IMPORTER_PORT=8030
# Host uid/gid for the non-root services' bind-mount writes. `make` exports the
# real values (id -u / id -g) which override these; for plain `docker compose`
# on a host whose uid/gid is NOT 1000, edit them to match `id -u` / `id -g`.
UID=1000
GID=1000

# dora_live cutover: see the "dora_live cutover (LIVE=1)" block above (robot-
# side placement, ruled 2026-07-22) — LIVE=1 there is the only knob; make
# derives the 8005/8006/8007 ports itself.
# 【日本語】dora_live 切替は上の「dora_live cutover (LIVE=1)」ブロック参照
# (2026-07-22 裁定でロボット側配置)。ノブは LIVE=1 のみ・ポートは make が導出。
